# Real-Time Audio Performance

This firmware has real microsecond-scale deadlines. Know the actual budget
before touching hot-path code — don't guess:

- CPU @ 550 MHz, sample rate 48 kHz → ~11,458 CPU cycles (~20.8 µs) of
  aggregate headroom per sample across everything running (ISRs, USB,
  console). Generation is batched, so this isn't a hard per-sample deadline,
  but it's the right mental model for "is this fast enough."
- **Tightest real deadline in this codebase**: the TIM7 UDR-guard
  (`I2S2_PumpTimerInit()` in `audio_bridge.c`) fires every **10 µs** and must
  clear the SPI2 underrun flag before the slave wedges. Use this as the
  reference example for "must complete in microseconds."
- The I2S1 half/full DMA callbacks have a **~1 ms** budget (48 frames
  @ 48 kHz) to refill half the ring buffer — enough for table lookups +
  interpolation at 16 voices, but no longer a multi-millisecond soft window.

## Hot path = ISRs, DMA callbacks, per-sample generators

(`Tone_NextSineSample()`, any new per-channel generator, `Audio_I2S2_Pump()`.)
In these:

- No heap allocation, no blocking HAL calls (`HAL_Delay`, blocking I2C/SPI
  transactions), no `printf`/console I/O.
- No floating point and no runtime 64-bit division — precompute divides
  outside the ISR, exactly as `TONE_PHASE_INC()` is computed once per
  frequency change instead of once per sample.
- Prefer `static inline` helpers over large monolithic ISR bodies — the
  compiler inlines them away (zero call overhead) while keeping the hot path
  short and single-purpose.
- When in doubt whether something is "fast enough," reason in cycles
  (`cycles = seconds × 550,000,000`) before micro-optimizing — the
  `Tone_NextSineSample()` comment ("~10 cycles/sample — negligible at 550
  MHz") is the model to follow.

## Interrupt priority is part of the contract

USB is deliberately higher priority than SAI/audio DMA on the Effect Card,
because missing an isochronous token loses a frame outright while audio
callbacks have a soft ~ms deadline. Any new ISR must be slotted into the
priority order deliberately, not left at its default.
